# The post-reload coherence case, run by the driver AFTER both reloads have
# landed and the old worker has drained. It proves the server came back
# HEALTHY: the final worker serves a correct 200, leaks no descriptor, and
# logs nothing on the error path in the window around this request.
#
# The pid oracle is STRICT (no pid_may_change), same rationale as
# hup-storm-mid-transfer's post-reload.rule: by the time this file runs a single
# post-reload worker is serving, so both probe snapshots see the SAME worker and
# "same worker pid" is the stronger, true assertion. The reload-while-compressing
# claims this scenario exists to make are the driver's own oracles (which
# straddle the two signals and decompress the held frame); this leg only proves
# the server came back healthy afterwards.
#
# Requests "/" (a plain 200, not the compressed drip path) on purpose: this leg
# is about server health after the reloads, and delta fds == 0 must reflect a
# normal request's descriptor accounting, not a memcached-upstream fetch.

name    the final worker serves cleanly after reloading mid-compression
from    127.0.0.1
send    GET / HTTP/1.1\r\n
send    Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
expect  status=200
delta   fds == 0
no_error_log  \[error\]
